statement:
`nums` holds `n` positive integers. Break every value into two non-negative parts, `arr1[i] + arr2[i] = nums[i]`, so that the array of first parts rises (never drops) from left to right and the array of second parts falls (never grows). Count the different pairs of arrays `(arr1, arr2)` that do this, and return the count modulo `10^9 + 7`.

The full set of rules for a pair:

- `arr1` and `arr2` both have length `n` and contain only integers `>= 0`;
- `arr1[i] + arr2[i] == nums[i]` at every index `i`;
- `arr1[0] <= arr1[1] <= ... <= arr1[n - 1]`;
- `arr2[0] >= arr2[1] >= ... >= arr2[n - 1]`.

validator:
function validate([nums]) {
  if (!Array.isArray(nums) || nums.length < 1 || nums.length > 2000) return 'nums needs 1 to 2000 values.';
  if (nums.some(v => !Number.isInteger(v) || v < 1 || v > 50)) return 'Each value of nums is an integer from 1 to 50.';
  return null;
}

